{
  "_comment": "Smart keyword expansion map for Job Radar ad-hoc searches. Seeded from CV-KM-2026 (CCIE-DC, Cisco DC/SDN/automation/validation). EDIT FREELY - this file is data, not code. Expansion is applied ONLY to ad-hoc searches (POST /api/scrape/adhoc); the daily config.json queries are untouched. The dashboard's job-list search box reads this file too (abbreviations, spellings, synonym titles).",

  "_howItWorks": [
    "1. The query is normalised: lowercased, accents folded to ASCII (inżynier -> inzynier, Fördertechnik -> fordertechnik), punctuation stripped, whitespace collapsed.",
    "2. Typos and plurals are corrected against every word in this file ('netwrok engineer' -> 'network engineer', 'engineers' -> 'engineer'). The query as typed is still searched first.",
    "3. 'abbreviations' expand ONE WAY (abbr -> full), so typing 'dc engineer' also searches 'data center engineer'. Entries listed in 'techAcronyms' still expand for matching, but their spelled-out form is a weak search term ('cisco application centric infrastructure'), so it ranks low.",
    "4. 'contractions' go the other way (full -> abbr) but only for forms employers genuinely publish. Kept as a separate allowlist because blanket contraction produces junk like 'network engr' that matches nothing and wastes a request against every portal.",
    "5. 'spellings' normalises equivalent spellings (center/centre) so one query covers both.",
    "6. 'synonyms' groups are bidirectional: matching ANY member (or one of its pl/de/nl titles) yields the members. A group matches when the query IS a member, CONTAINS a member as whole words ('cloud network engineer' contains 'network engineer'), or - only for a short topic that is not itself a job title - is contained in a member ('devops' -> 'devops engineer'). A recognised title no longer drags in every group that merely mentions it.",
    "7. 'modifiers' are seniority/qualifier prefixes that are stripped for matching and re-applied to the expansions, so 'senior network engineer' searches 'senior network administrator' rather than the bare title.",
    "8. A query without a role noun ('roleNouns') becomes a title too: 'kubernetes' -> 'kubernetes engineer'. Certifications ('certifications') are not turned into titles ('ccie engineer' is not a posting).",
    "9. '_vendorHints' and 'familyHints' map technologies and certifications to a role family; they are used only when no synonym group matched. _vendorHints also feeds the CV keyword chips (cv-keywords.ts); familyHints is expansion-only.",
    "10. Every candidate is scored by how close it is to the query (exact family > contained title > topic match > vendor hint, plus word overlap), and the best MAX_EXPANSIONS (12) are kept.",
    "11. 'pl' / 'de' / 'nl' on a synonym group are native-language titles for that family. Up to 2 per language are added ON TOP of the cap; the dashboard only searches the ones whose market is in the selected scope (Poland -> pl, Germany -> de, Netherlands -> nl, Everywhere -> all, EU remote -> none). They are folded to ASCII because the scraper only accepts ASCII arguments."
  ],

